  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Investment preparation study Nigeria</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Closing the Loop B.V. (CTL) provides waste compensation services that enable them to make electronic devices such as telephones, laptops and computers &apos;waste neutral&apos;. A small fee, less than 1% of the price, is charged for each new device purchased by organisations. This fee is used by CTL to collect and sort discarded end-of-life electronics in countries where waste is normally poorly managed and is often incinerated in an unsafe manner. The collection and recycling of this waste thus compensates for the new device. Waste compensation for electronic devices is an international financing mechanism that enables European and Western companies that often buy thousands of devices per year to purchase their electronics sustainably.CTL has already organised the collection of waste electronics in a number of African countries, including Nigeria, and has thus made an important contribution to the establishment of formal and legal collection structures. Because there is currently no safe recycling capacity in Africa, the collected and sorted waste is now shipped to Italy and melted down into raw materials there. Because shipping to Italy and melting it down in Italy is expensive for CTL, the company wants to process the waste in Africa.CTL wants to set up a recycling plant in Lagos, with a high-quality smelter for melting down precious metals from end-of-life electronics. The metals recovered, such as copper, gold, silver, etc, can then be refined and used again as a raw material. This project will further professionalise the recycling sector in Nigeria, which will help to formalise the supply chain and reduce illegal activities.CTL wants to make the investment together with a number of third financing parties.</narrative>
  </description>
